{
  "id": "u06-l02",
  "unit": "u06-bazaar",
  "title": "Taka & prices",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Prices are just **number + টাকা** (*taka*): **পঞ্চাশ টাকা** 'fifty taka'. With no 'is' in the present, **চা দশ টাকা** means 'Tea (is) ten taka'. The sign is **৳**; in English you'll see *Tk 50*.\n\nHundreds are built with **-শ** (*-sho*): **একশ** 100, **দুইশ** 200, **পাঁচশ** 500. A thousand is **হাজার**: **এক হাজার টাকা** = ৳1,000. Price tags may use Bengali digits: ৫০ = 50.\n\nWatch the look-alikes:\n\n- **পঞ্চাশ** 50 / **পনেরো** 15 / **পাঁচশ** 500\n- **দুইশ** 200 / **দুইটা** 'two (things)'\n- **দশ টাকা** 'ten taka' / **দশটা** 'ten o'clock'\n\nA taka is 100 **পয়সা** (*poysha*). **শুধু** (*shudhu*) = 'only': **শুধু পঞ্চাশ টাকা**.\n\n*Culture:* small vendors and rickshaw drivers often can't break a ৳500 or ৳1,000 note, so keep small notes handy. If you miss a price, just ask again: **কত?**",
  "vocab": [
    {
      "id": "taka",
      "bn": "টাকা",
      "tr": "taka",
      "en": "taka",
      "notes": "Bangladesh's currency (৳, BDT). Also the general word for 'money': টাকা নেই 'no money'. In India টাকা means rupees."
    },
    {
      "id": "poysha",
      "bn": "পয়সা",
      "tr": "poysha",
      "en": "poisha",
      "notes": "1/100 of a taka. Coins are now rare, but you hear it in sayings and in counting."
    },
    {
